`payload.update(self._settings.extra)` runs after the service sets `messages`, `conversation_id`, and `conversation_require_cache`. That means any runtime `extra` key can overwrite protocol-owned fields, so the code can send `conversation_require_cache` on a full-history request, omit it on a suffix request, inject a `conversation_id` even when cache is supposed to be disabled, or replace `messages` while `_process_context()` still promotes `committed_messages = full_messages` as if the original transcript was what went over the wire.

2. High — [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:958](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:958), [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1045](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1045)  
`model` is sent on every request but is not part of `cache_shape_fingerprint`. If the model changes via settings update mid-conversation, the next append-only turn will still look cache-compatible and can send a suffix with `conversation_require_cache=True` under the old `conversation_id` instead of rotating. Model identity is part of cache-attach semantics, so this violates the step-5 contract.

3. Medium — [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1029](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1029), [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1095](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1095), [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1919](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1919)  
Rotation clears `_turn_tool_results` even when `_current_turn_user_key` has not changed. So a same-user-turn rotation caused by tool-schema / tool-choice / prompt-shape change between tool rounds drops the duplicate-command guard and allows the next round to re-run an exact `run_bash` command instead of returning `duplicate_suppressed`.

4. Low — [tests/test_nemotron_omni_aligned.py:1994](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/tests/test_nemotron_omni_aligned.py:1994), [src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1567](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-nano-omni/src/nemotron_voice/services/nvidia/nemotron_omni.py:1567)  
The new rebase tests inject `ConversationCacheMissError` directly from the fake `_stream_completion_pass`, so they never exercise the real HTTP-409 parser in `_is_conversation_cache_miss()` or the real `_stream_completion_pass()` miss path. I did not find a parser bug by inspection, but this part of the contract is currently uncovered.

**Verified-sound items**
- Absent `settings.extra` clobbering, the suffix branch and `conversation_require_cache` branch use the same predicate, so suffix/full-history selection is internally consistent.
- Promotion is correctly gated on `result is not None`, and the promoted value is `full_messages`, not generated assistant output.
- Cancelled/failed requests leave `committed_messages`, `_conversation_cache_committed`, and `_committed_cache_shape_fingerprint` untouched.
- The one-shot miss→rebase path keeps the same `conversation_id`, swaps in full `full_messages`, removes `conversation_require_cache`, and a second miss exits the retry loop instead of spinning.
- The followup tool re-entry shape looks correct by inspection: after a committed `[..., user]` prefix, the next request projects `[assistant(tool_calls), tool...]`; the interrupted-tool case projects only the new user row.

Verification note: static review only; `pytest` is not installed in this environment.

Verdict: not ready as-is; the core suffix/rebase logic is mostly sound, but the `settings.extra` overwrite hole and the missing model-based rotation are both contract-breaking.
